## 7. Optimized Solution with Sets / Bitmasking

### Using HashSets (Clean approach)

Three sets track used columns and diagonals:
```java
Set<Integer> cols = new HashSet<>();
Set<Integer> diag1 = new HashSet<>();  // r - c
Set<Integer> diag2 = new HashSet<>();  // r + c
```
- Add before recursing, remove after (backtrack).
- O(1) lookup per validity check.

### Using Bitmasks (Fastest approach)

Replace sets with integer bitmasks — each bit represents one column/diagonal:
```java
int colMask, diag1Mask, diag2Mask;
// Check: (colMask >> col) & 1 == 0  (bit not set = safe)
// Set:   colMask |= (1 << col)
// Clear: colMask &= ~(1 << col)
```
Advantage: bitwise operations are extremely fast; no object allocation.

