## 14. Recursion Tree — Sudoku

A partial view of the decision tree for one empty cell:

```
                  f(cell=(0,2), board)
                        │
              Try digits 1 through 9:
                        │
    ┌──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┐
    1      2      3      4     ...     9
   (✗)    (✗)   (✓)    (✗)           (✗)
           │      │
          ...   f(cell=(0,5))
                   │
             Try digits 1–9:
                   │
           ┌──────┬──────┬──────┐
           1      2     ...     9
          (✓)    (✗)          (✗)
           │
        f(next empty cell)
           │
          ...
           │
        f(row=9) → SOLVED ✅ return true

If any cell returns false (all 9 digits exhausted):
    ← backtrack: reset cell to '.', try next digit in parent
```

### Search Space vs Pruning

```
Worst case without pruning:
  Up to 81 empty cells × 9 choices each = 9^81 possibilities
  (astronomically large)

With Sudoku constraints:
  Most cells have very few valid options
  In practice, typical puzzles solve in microseconds
  The backtracking tree is heavily pruned by constraints
```

