# PART 3 — INTERVIEW PREPARATION

## 17. Comparison: N-Queens vs Sudoku

```
┌──────────────────────┬────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┐
│ Aspect               │ N-Queens                │ Sudoku Solver            │
├──────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
│ Grid size            │ N × N (variable)        │ Fixed 9 × 9              │
│ Traverse by          │ Row by row              │ Cell by cell (row,col)   │
│ Choices per step     │ N columns               │ Digits 1–9               │
│ Validity checks      │ col, diag1, diag2       │ row, col, 3×3 box        │
│ Find how many?       │ All solutions           │ One solution (stop early)│
│ Return type          │ List of boards          │ Modify board in place    │
│ Pruning              │ 3 constraint sets       │ 3 constraint arrays      │
│ Backtrack operation  │ Remove queen, unmark    │ Reset cell to '.', unmark│
│ Base case            │ row == N                │ row == 9                 │
│ Time complexity      │ O(N!)                   │ O(9^M) where M = empties │
└──────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┘
```

### Both share the same core pattern:

```
1. Find next position to fill
2. Try all valid choices
3. For each valid choice:
   a. Apply choice
   b. Recurse
   c. If successful → return (Sudoku) or continue (N-Queens)
   d. Undo choice (backtrack)
4. Return failure if no choice worked
```

