## 19. Interview Preparation Guide

### 🎯 Recognition Signals

**N-Queens**: Any problem involving placing N non-attacking pieces on a grid.
**Sudoku**: Any constraint satisfaction problem on a 2D grid with row/col/region rules.

Both signal: **"Use backtracking with constraint checking."**

---

### 🗣️ How to Explain N-Queens in an Interview

**Step 1 — Observation (10 seconds)**
> "We place one queen per row. That's N decisions, each choosing a column. This is a backtracking tree with branching factor N."

**Step 2 — State the approach (20 seconds)**
> "I'll recurse row by row. At each row, I try each column. Before placing, I check three constraints: no shared column, no shared primary diagonal (r-c constant), no shared anti-diagonal (r+c constant). I'll track these in three sets for O(1) lookups."

**Step 3 — Explain backtracking (10 seconds)**
> "If placing a queen leads to a dead end in a later row, I remove it and try the next column. This is the backtrack step."

**Step 4 — Complexity (10 seconds)**
> "Time is O(N!) since we're generating all permutations of queen placements. Space is O(N) for the recursion stack and constraint sets."

---

### 🗣️ How to Explain Sudoku in an Interview

**Step 1 — Observation (10 seconds)**
> "This is a constraint satisfaction problem. We fill empty cells one by one, trying digits 1–9. If a placement violates any constraint, we backtrack."

**Step 2 — State the approach (20 seconds)**
> "I'll scan the board left-to-right, top-to-bottom. At each empty cell, I try digits 1–9. For each digit, I validate against the row, column, and 3×3 box. If valid, I place it and recurse to the next cell. If recursion returns true, I'm done. Otherwise, I reset and try the next digit."

**Step 3 — Optimization (10 seconds)**
> "For performance, I can use boolean arrays — `rowUsed[9][10]`, `colUsed[9][10]`, `boxUsed[9][10]` — to make validity checks O(1) instead of O(9)."

**Step 4 — Complexity (10 seconds)**
> "Worst case O(9^M) where M is empty cells, but in practice the constraint pruning makes it extremely fast."

